# rozier-piecepack

[![DOI](https://zenodo.org/badge/DOI/10.5281/zenodo.24543880.svg)](https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.24543880)

A fallback layout search for Qiskit. It finds zero-swap layouts that the stock `VF2Layout` pass misses when a circuit's two-qubit interaction graph is split into several medium-sized pieces and nearly every qubit on the chip is in use.

Background and data: [Qiskit issue #17609](https://github.com/Qiskit/qiskit/issues/17609).

## The problem in one paragraph

On IBM's 156-qubit heavy-hex (Heron r2) graph, `transpile(optimization_level=3)` sometimes reports `VF2Layout` `NO_SOLUTION_FOUND` for circuits that fit the chip with zero swaps. It then falls back to SabreLayout. The miss costs 17-36 s of VF2 search. With the chip's own qubit numbering, Sabre usually still lands on a zero-swap layout, so only time is lost. With any other numbering, the result can have ~2.4x the two-qubit gates. On chip-numbered misses a second pass, `VF2PostLayout`, then repeats the same hard search, roughly doubling the compile time. It shows up for ordinary circuits. One example is Ising Trotter steps on a fixed subset of ~70-85% of the couplers, which is near heavy-hex's bond-percolation point: almost every qubit is active, but the pattern breaks into several medium pieces.

## How it works

When stock `VF2Layout` reports `NO_SOLUTION_FOUND`, `transpile_with_fallback` stops the stock run at that point (early exit). That skips the Sabre fallback, and on chip-numbered circuits it also skips a `VF2PostLayout` search that can take as long as the miss itself. It then searches the exact interaction graph that `VF2Layout` saw, in stages:

0. **Quick piece-pack try (1 s).** Most piece-pack wins take well under a second.
1. **Round-trip `VF2Layout` retry (2 s).** A fresh DAG gives Qiskit's canonical edge order, and Qiskit scores the layout by error.
2. **Whole-chip VF2, VF2++ order (2 s).** Keeps the lowest-error of up to 50 layouts.
3. **Whole-chip VF2, node-index order (2 s).** Same scoring.
4. **Piece-pack with all the time left.**

Stages 1-3 cover the case where piece-pack is weakest: many small components.

**Piece-pack** places whole pieces instead of single qubits. Plain VF2 grows a match one qubit at a time and prunes by checking each new qubit against qubits already placed. A piece that isn't connected to anything placed yet has nothing to be checked against, so a bad spot is often found only when the chip is nearly full. Piece-pack instead works like this:

- Pieces are placed biggest first, each with rustworkx VF2 on the chip's still-free qubits.
- After every placement it checks a cheap necessary condition: can the leftover free regions still hold the leftover pieces? For each size *t*, the pieces of size ≥ *t* must fit into the sum over regions of ⌊region / *t*⌋ slots. This is forward checking in the constraint-satisfaction sense.
- Placements covering exactly the same qubits as one already tried are skipped.

If a zero-swap layout is found, the circuit is compiled with it as `initial_layout`. If nothing is found, the circuit is compiled with `layout_method="sabre"`, the same method stock falls back to. So the result is never worse than stock, and `VF2Layout` doesn't run twice. When `VF2Layout` succeeds, nothing extra runs.

## Install

```
pip install git+https://github.com/catrozier08-gif/rozier-piecepack     # until it is on PyPI
pip install "rozier-piecepack[qiskit]"                                   # once on PyPI
```

## Use

```python
from qiskit_ibm_runtime.fake_provider import FakeKingston
from rozier_piecepack import transpile_with_fallback

target = FakeKingston().target
out, info = transpile_with_fallback(circuit, target, optimization_level=3, seed_transpiler=1)
print(info["vf2_stop"], info["search"], info["stage"], info["used"], info["final_2q"])
```

`early_exit=False` lets the stock run finish and keeps whichever result has fewer two-qubit gates.

In a custom pass manager, `PiecePackLayout(target=..., time_limit=...)` is an analysis pass that runs the same staged search and sets `property_set["layout"]` when it finds a zero-swap layout. By default it does nothing if a layout is already set. Its outcome is recorded in `property_set["PiecePackLayout_stop_reason"]`.

The searches alone need only rustworkx:

```python
from rozier_piecepack import piece_pack, check_mapping
mapping, status, stats = piece_pack(chip_graph, pattern_graph, time_limit=10)   # status: FOUND / TIMEOUT / NONE
# also: whole_chip_vf2(chip_graph, pattern_graph, time_limit=2, id_order=False, score=...)
```

## Results

Run with `examples/boundary_benchmark.py` (v0.2.1). Setup: Qiskit 2.6.2, rustworkx 0.17.1, FakeKingston, 30 s limit. Stock and `transpile_with_fallback` are compiled side by side in the same session. The 16 circuits are the ones where stock level-3 `VF2Layout` returned `NO_SOLUTION_FOUND`, out of 48 Ising Trotter circuits on 65-90% of the couplers.

| | stock level 3 | with fallback | zero-swap |
|---|---|---|---|
| zero-swap layout found | 0/16 | **14/16** | 16/16 by construction |
| 2q gates, all 16 | 14780 | **8738** | 8738 |
| 2q gates, 8 with shuffled numbering | 10394 | **4352** | 4352 |
| compile time, chip numbering (median) | 63.4 s | **41.5 s** | |
| compile time, shuffled numbering (median) | 32.1 s | **30.8 s** | |
| total compile time, all 16 | 728 s | **588 s** | |

- **Every miss that cost gates was fixed**, and no circuit ended with more gates than stock. 0 invalid layouts.
- **Search time: median 0.76 s, max 23.3 s.** Found by: piece-pack 10, whole-chip VF2 (id order) 2, whole-chip VF2++ 1, round-trip `VF2Layout` 1.
- **Chip-numbered circuits were never slower than stock**, thanks to the early exit.
- **Three shuffled-numbering circuits took longer than stock** (by 7-24 s). These are the hard searches, and each one also cut the two-qubit gates by more than half (for example 1336 → 554).
- **The 2 not solved are chip-numbered** (pieces 49 28 16 11 … and 51 25 20 17 …). Stock's Sabre fallback already reaches their zero-swap gate count, so they cost time but not gates. Here they finish in about stock's time (55.9 vs 57.8 s, 66.2 vs 74.6 s).

v0.1.0, piece-pack only with no early exit: 13/16 found, median search 0.45 s, and the compile waited for the full stock run.

## Limits (read before relying on it)

- **Tested on heavy-hex only:** FakeKingston (Heron r2, 156 q) and synthetic `CouplingMap.from_heavy_hex` graphs. Other topologies are untested.
- **Error-aware only in part.** The whole-chip stages keep the lowest-error of up to 50 layouts, and the round-trip stage uses Qiskit's own scoring. Piece-pack returns the first valid layout it finds.
- **The time limit is approximate.** Each VF2 call gets a budget sized to the time left, assuming about 1e6 VF2 states per second.
- **`NONE` is not a proof** that no layout exists, only that none was found within the budget.
- **Some cases are still out of reach.** Two circuits stayed unsolved at 30 s. Two large pieces of similar size remain the hardest pattern.
- **Default time limit is 30 s.** A search that succeeds returns early, so the limit only matters on the hard cases, and stock `VF2Layout` has usually already spent 20-40 s on those.
- **Early exit stops the stock run from inside a pass-manager callback.** If a future Qiskit version changes how callbacks work, use `early_exit=False`; the tests include a check for this.

## Related work

- VF2 (Cordella, Foggia, Sansone, Vento, 2004) and VF2++ (Jüttner & Madarasi, 2018), as used by rustworkx.
- Heavy-tailed run times in backtracking search, with restarts (Gomes, Selman, Crato, Kautz, 2000; Luby, Sinclair, Zuckerman, 1993).
- Forward checking in constraint satisfaction (Haralick & Elliott, 1980).
- Bond percolation on lattices: the coupler fraction where heavy-hex breaks into pieces.

